# Code Standards

### Modules in BeyondJS

BeyondJS introduces the concept of a **module**, which is a collection of files grouped together by a `module.json`
file. Since BeyondJS runs on a DevServer, each module is packaged and consumed as a **single file**.

When importing a module, it must always be consumed using the same **bare specifier**, which combines the package name
and the module name (subpath). For example:

```
pragmate-ui/components
```

Here:

-   `pragmate-ui` → the package name
-   `components` → the module name (or subpath)

BeyondJS also defines standard TypeScript/JavaScript files as **internal modules**. These internal modules can only use
relative imports **within the same module**. They cannot be imported outside of their module unless explicitly exposed
with the special comment:

```ts
/*bundle*/
```

Once exposed, they can then be imported using the bare specifier as explained above.

## Module Import Rules

**CRITICAL**: All files with `/*bundle*/` within a module (defined by module.json) are imported using the same
bare-specifier, regardless of their location within the module folder.

**Rule**: One module = One bare-specifier for all its `/*bundle*/` exports. Never use relative imports for cross-module
dependencies.

## Core Principles

-   Don't create additional code or infered content, only what is gived as instruction.
-   **No style imports in TypeScript/JavaScript files** - build system handles this automatically
-   **No @import in SCSS files** - use `@use` instead, without file extensions
-   **Modular, tool-agnostic JavaScript/TypeScript** with universal design
-   **Plain named imports**; no default exports
-   **CamelCase** for variables, methods, and properties; interfaces prefixed with `I`
-   **Logic extraction**: All logic must be extracted from JSX returns (in React code) and kept outside return
    statements
-   **File naming**: Use hyphens (e.g., `folder-name/file-name`)
-   **Styling**: Use Sass/CSS custom properties by default
-   **Naming conventions**: Avoid duplicate wording in object/class naming (e.g., class `Handler` must not have methods
    named `handle*`)
-   **Dependencies**: Only include library dependencies when explicitly requested or required

## Code Generation Guidelines

-   **Do not generate code** unless explicitly asked or necessary
-   **Respond with conceptual explanations** unless code is explicitly requested
-   **When code is requested**: Use concise, semantically clear TypeScript

## TypeScript Standards

-   Use strong typing; avoid `any`
-   Prefer explicit return types for functions
-   Use interfaces for object shapes (prefixed with `I`)
-   Utilize type guards and proper type narrowing

## React Component Structure

```typescript
// ✅ Correct structure - Use regular functions, not arrow functions
function ComponentName(props: IComponentProps): JSX.Element {
	// All logic here, outside return
	const handleClick = (): void => {
		// Logic
	};

	const computedValue = useMemo(() => {
		// Computations
	}, [dependencies]);

	return <div>{/* Clean JSX only */}</div>;
}

// ❌ Never import styles in React components
// import './component.scss';  // Wrong!
// import './styles.css';      // Wrong!
```

## Import/Export Standards

```typescript
// ✅ Correct - named imports/exports
import { ComponentName, IComponentProps } from './component-name';
export { ComponentName, IComponentProps };

// ❌ Avoid - default exports
import ComponentName from './component-name';
export default ComponentName;
```

## Style Import Standards

### **TypeScript/JavaScript Files**

```typescript
// ❌ Never import CSS/SCSS files in TS/JS
import './styles.scss'; // Wrong
import './component.css'; // Wrong
import './styles'; // Wrong

// ✅ Styles are handled by the build system
// No style imports needed
```

### **SCSS Files**

```scss
// ❌ Avoid @import (deprecated)
@import 'variables'; // Wrong
@import 'mixins.scss'; // Wrong

// ✅ Use @use (modern approach)
@use 'variables'; // Correct
@use 'mixins'; // Correct
@use 'theme/colors'; // Correct

// ✅ No file extensions with @use
@use 'variables'; // Correct
@use 'variables.scss'; // Wrong
```

## Module Export Guidelines

-   **No index files**: Do not create `index.ts` files for internal module organization
-   **Use magic comment for external consumption**: If a component or object needs to be consumed from other modules,
    add the magic comment `/*bundle*/` to the export
-   **Direct imports**: Import directly from the specific file within the module

## Style Management

-   **Build system handles styles**: CSS/SCSS files are automatically processed by the build system
-   **No manual imports**: Never manually import style files in TypeScript/JavaScript
-   **SCSS organization**: Use `@use` for sharing variables, mixins, and functions between SCSS files
-   **File extensions**: Omit file extensions when using `@use` in SCSS files

```typescript
// ✅ Correct - Direct import with magic comment for external use
export /*bundle*/ function MyComponent(): JSX.Element {
	// Component implementation
}

// ✅ Correct - Direct import within same module
import { MyComponent } from './my-component';

// ❌ Avoid - Creating index files for internal organization
// Don't create: ./index.ts
```

## File and Folder Naming

-   Use kebab-case for files and folders: `user-profile.tsx`, `api-client/`
-   Component files: `component-name.tsx`
-   Utility files: `utility-name.ts`
-   Type files: `types.ts` or `component-name.types.ts`

## Style File Naming

-   Component styles: `component-name.scss` (match component name)
-   Page styles: `page-name.scss` (match page name)
-   Global styles: `global.scss`, `variables.scss`, `mixins.scss`
-   **Never include file extensions in @use statements**

## Styling Standards

-   Use Sass with CSS custom properties
-   Avoid inline styles
-   Use semantic class names
-   Leverage CSS custom properties for theming
-   **No CSS/Sass imports in TypeScript/JavaScript files**
-   **No CSS/Sass imports in SCSS files** - use `@use` instead
-   **No file extensions** when using `@use` in SCSS files

## Naming Conventions

```typescript
// ✅ Correct naming
interface IUserProfile {
	firstName: string;
	lastName: string;
}

class UserService {
	getUserData(): IUserProfile {
		// Implementation
	}
}

// ❌ Avoid duplicate wording
class Handler {
	// ❌ Don't use "handle" in method names
	handleRequest(): void {}

	// ✅ Use descriptive action names
	processRequest(): void {}
}

// ❌ Avoid redundant naming in module context
// If a module is named "components", don't repeat "components" in internal files
// ❌ components-sidebar.tsx, components-content.tsx
// ✅ sidebar.tsx, content.tsx

// ❌ Avoid redundant naming in class context
// If a class is named "UserManager", don't repeat "User" in method names
// ❌ userManager.getUserData(), userManager.createUser()
// ✅ userManager.getData(), userManager.create()
```

**Important Notes:**

-   The `model` parameter is **required** in the constructor - an exception will be thrown if not provided
-   The model instance is created in the `load()` method, not in the constructor
-   The `load()` method accepts an optional `id` parameter that will be set on the model before loading
